TL;DR calling the `MemberInfo.GetCustomAttributes()` overload that doesn't accept an `attributeType` parameter can be expensive, as this benchmark demonstrates:
```C#

using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;
using BenchmarkDotNet.Running;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;
using System.Runtime.Serialization;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

BenchmarkRunner.Run<MyBench>(args: args);

public class MyBench
{
    private readonly Type _type = typeof(SimpleStructWithProperties);
    private readonly Type _requiredMemberAttribute = typeof(RequiredMemberAttribute);

    [Benchmark]
    public RequiredMemberAttribute? GetCustomAttributeNoTypeArgument()
    {
        foreach (object attribute in _type.GetCustomAttributes(inherit: false))
        {
            if (attribute.GetType() == _requiredMemberAttribute)
            {
                return (RequiredMemberAttribute)attribute;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public RequiredMemberAttribute? GetCustomAttributeTypeArgument()
    {
        return (RequiredMemberAttribute?)_type.GetCustomAttributes(_requiredMemberAttribute, inherit: false).FirstOrDefault();
    }

    [DataContract]
    [JsonConverter(typeof(int))]
    public struct SimpleStructWithProperties
    {
        public required int Num { get; set; }
        public string Text { get; set; }
    }
}
```
Giving
```
|                           Method |       Mean |    Error |   StdDev |
|--------------------------------- |-----------:|---------:|---------:|
| GetCustomAttributeNoTypeArgument | 2,874.3 ns | 45.26 ns | 42.33 ns |
|   GetCustomAttributeTypeArgument |   510.3 ns |  9.71 ns | 14.82 ns |
```
The problem is that we can't use the `attributeType` overload [in this particular use case](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/0d6d6c343556d8aaae49347c82b09281f8968196/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/src/System/ReflectionExtensions.cs#L46-L70) since we need to account for compiler-generated polyfills. I'll see if I can come up with a mitigation that addresses this particular performance regression.

cc @dotnet/area-system-reflection @stephentoub if you can recommend a workaround for less expensive attribute resolution.

> I assume this is one-time use only and the results are cached in some form.

Correct, that particular benchmark measures serialization performance without any caches available.

FWIW, here's an updated variation of the benchmark targeting `System.ValueType` which was the actual type causing the regression:

```C#
using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;
using BenchmarkDotNet.Running;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

BenchmarkRunner.Run<MyBench>(args: args);

public class MyBench
{
    private readonly Type _type = typeof(ValueType);
    private readonly Type _requiredMemberAttribute = typeof(RequiredMemberAttribute);

    [Benchmark]
    public RequiredMemberAttribute? GetCustomAttributeNoTypeArgument()
    {
        foreach (object attribute in _type.GetCustomAttributes(inherit: false))
        {
            if (attribute.GetType() == _requiredMemberAttribute)
            {
                return (RequiredMemberAttribute)attribute;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public RequiredMemberAttribute? GetCustomAttributeTypeArgument()
    {
        return (RequiredMemberAttribute?)_type.GetCustomAttributes(_requiredMemberAttribute, inherit: false).FirstOrDefault();
    }
}
```

|                           Method |       Mean |   Error |  StdDev |
|--------------------------------- |-----------:|--------:|--------:|
| GetCustomAttributeNoTypeArgument | 1,916.3 ns | 7.97 ns | 7.45 ns |
|   GetCustomAttributeTypeArgument |   391.8 ns | 0.44 ns | 0.39 ns |
